I’ll inspect the measurement metadata and diffraction pattern first, then use the installed crystallographic/indexing tools to identify candidate phases and carry the best model through quantitative refinement. I’ll preserve the final refinement project and export all requested artifacts from that same model.

The chemistry points toward calcium tungstate as the principal reaction product, but 500 °C can leave precursor or intermediate phases, so I’m treating that only as a hypothesis. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the available reference-search/refinement interfaces before assigning phases.

Peak matching shows a consistent ≈+0.30° 2θ displacement across the strongest lines—an instrumental/specimen-position effect, not a lattice change. After correcting for it, the dominant 23–25° triplet is characteristic of monoclinic WO₃; CaWO₄ and calcite each account for separate secondary line families. I’m comparing alternative polymorph CIFs quantitatively before refinement.

The first staged Rietveld run is underway. I’m releasing scale/background, displacement, cells, and line broadening in separate cycles so the strong instrumental shift does not get absorbed into false lattice changes; the final fractions will be normalized only after convergence.

The three-phase model is now strongly supported and reaches Rwp ≈9.4% before phase-specific broadening. Letting every phase broaden independently lowered Rwp further but produced nonphysical parameters (including a negative microstrain and a 1 nm apparent domain size), so I’m rejecting that overfit and returning to the physically defensible common-profile solution. Next I’m checking whether the remaining residuals reflect texture/atomic intensities or a missing phase.

The residual contains no coherent unmatched peak family: its largest features sit on already indexed WO₃/calcite/CaWO₄ reflections and are line-shape or relative-intensity mismatches. A modest calcite [104] March–Dollase texture term improves Rwp from 9.39% to 9.27% and remains physical; I’m using that model, while treating the difference from the texture-free model as a systematic fraction uncertainty rather than reporting only optimistic least-squares errors.

The final model and exports are now written from the same GSAS-II project. I’m doing a consistency pass: reopening `final.gpx`, recomputing its mass fractions, validating all three exported CIFs, and checking that `fit.csv` exactly matches the project arrays and `result.json` sums to unity.

Identified three crystalline phases:

- Monoclinic WO₃: 55.56 wt%
- Calcite CaCO₃: 36.23 wt%
- Scheelite CaWO₄: 8.21 wt%

Fractions are normalized over crystalline phases. Final fit: Rwp 9.274%, Rp 6.727%, GOF 3.882. Realistic systematic fraction uncertainty is approximately ±2–3 percentage points.

